.TH BZIP2 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
bzip2, bunzip2, bzcat \- compress or decompress files in the bzip2 format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bzip2
.RB [ \-cfkqsvz ]
.RB [ \-1 " ... " \-9 ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B bzip2
.BR \-d | \-t
.RB [ \-cfkqv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B bunzip2
.RB [ \-cdfkqtv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B bzcat
.RB [ \-cdfkqtv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bzip2
compresses files in the bzip2 format, which sorts blocks of input with
the Burrows-Wheeler transform and then applies Huffman coding.
.B bunzip2
decompresses them, and
.B bzcat
decompresses them to standard output.
.B bunzip2
is the same as
.BR "bzip2 \-d" ,
and
.B bzcat
is the same as
.BR "bunzip2 \-c" .
.PP
Each
.I file
is replaced:
.B bzip2
appends
.B .bz2
to its name, and
.B bunzip2
removes that suffix.
The input file is deleted once its output is complete.
.B bunzip2
skips a name that does not end in
.BR .bz2 ,
reporting
.BR "unknown suffix \- ignored" .
An existing output file is an error unless
.B \-f
is given.
.PP
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
the command reads standard input and writes standard output.
With
.BR \-c ,
and always for
.BR bzcat ,
all output goes to standard output, the input files are kept, and
compressed file names need not end in
.BR .bz2 .
.PP
When a file fails, the error is reported on standard error, the partial
output file is removed, the input file is kept, and the remaining files
are still processed.
Output already written to standard output remains.
Reading standard input from a console without
.BR \-f ,
or failing to delete an input file, ends the command at once.
.PP
The options
.B \-1
to
.B \-9
set the block size to 100,000 to 900,000 bytes.
Larger blocks usually compress better and need more memory.
The block size is recorded in the compressed data, so decompression
needs no option.
.PP
Concatenated bzip2 streams, such as the output of several
.B bzip2 \-c
commands appended to one file, decompress as one.
Data after the last stream is ignored unless it begins with
.BR BZ ,
which is then an error.
.SS Diagnostics
Damaged input is reported with one of these messages:
.TP
.B invalid magic
The input does not begin with
.BR BZ .
.TP
.B CRC error
The checksum of a block or of the whole stream does not match.
For a stream with no data, the file still counts as a success.
.TP
.BI "bunzip error " n
.I n
is
.B \-2
for a damaged stream or block header,
.B \-3
for a truncated or unreadable file,
.B \-5
for corrupt data, or
.B \-7
for a block in the obsolete randomized format.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-kv ,
and may follow file operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
There are no long options.
.B bunzip2
and
.B bzcat
accept only
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-q ,
.BR \-t ,
and
.BR \-v ,
as does
.B bzip2
when
.B \-d
or
.B \-t
is given.
.TP
.BR \-1 " ... " \-9
Compress with a block size of the given multiple of 100,000 bytes.
The default is
.BR \-9 .
When several are given, the lowest wins.
.TP
.B \-d
Decompress.
.B bunzip2
and
.B bzcat
ignore it.
.TP
.B \-c
Write to standard output and keep the input files.
Compressing several files produces a series of streams, which
decompress as one.
.TP
.B \-f
Remove an existing output file before writing it, and read standard
input even when it is a console.
Without
.BR \-f ,
reading standard input from a console fails with
.BR "compressed data not read from terminal" .
.TP
.B \-k
Keep the input files.
.TP
.B \-t
Test: decompress each file and discard the output, keeping the input
files.
The
.B .bz2
suffix is not required.
Overrides
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B \-q
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.B \-s
The same as
.BR \-2 .
.TP
.B \-v
After converting each
.I file
to an output file, print its name, a percentage, and the output name
on standard error, even with
.BR \-k .
When decompressing, the percentage is the space saved by compression,
or a huge number if the compressed file is the larger.
When compressing, it is computed wrongly and is usually a huge number.
Nothing is printed for standard input, or with
.B \-c
or
.BR \-t .
.TP
.B \-z
Compress.
It has no effect, since
.B bzip2
compresses unless given
.B \-d
or
.BR \-t .
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit.
The message omits some options.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the command line are expanded at startup, for callers such
as
.B cmd.exe
that do not expand them.
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell do not expand
their arguments again.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
An octal file mode creation mask applied at startup.
If it includes the owner write bit,
.BR 0200 ,
output files are created read-only.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, options must precede the file operands.
.PP
Startup and messages written to a console also depend on
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid option, or an error with any file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compress a file, replacing it with
.IR notes.txt.bz2 :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ bzip2 notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decompress a file and report the space saved:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ bunzip2 \-v numbers.txt.bz2
numbers.txt.bz2: 77% \- replaced with numbers.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check the integrity of all compressed files in a directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ bzip2 \-t *.bz2
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compress with the smallest blocks, keeping the original:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ bzip2 \-c \-1 build.log >build.log.bz2
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compress two files into one, then decompress the result:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ bzip2 \-c part1.txt part2.txt >whole.bz2
$ bzcat whole.bz2 >whole.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the contents of a compressed tar archive:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ bzcat src.tar.bz2 | tar \-tf \-
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from bzip2 1.0:
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options, such as
.BR \-\-stdout ,
and no
.B \-L
or
.BR \-V .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-s
selects 200,000-byte blocks when compressing, and is rejected when
decompressing, rather than reducing the memory used to decompress.
.IP \(bu 2
Of several level options, the lowest wins, not the last.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-q
has no effect, and
.B \-v
prints a different report, only for files converted to an output
file.
.IP \(bu 2
.B bunzip2
recognizes only the
.B .bz2
suffix, compared case-sensitively, so
.I FILE.BZ2
is skipped.
It does not handle the
.BR .bz ,
.BR .tbz ,
or
.B .tbz2
suffixes, and does not decompress other names to
.IB name .out\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
.B bzip2
compresses a file even if its name already ends in
.BR .bz2 .
.IP \(bu 2
The output file gets the current time, not the input's modification
time.
.IP \(bu 2
Standard input is not read from a console without
.BR \-f ,
when compressing as well as when decompressing, but compressed data is
written to a console without complaint.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B BZIP2
and
.B BZIP
environment variables are ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
Blocks in the obsolete randomized format are rejected.
.PP
Standard input and output are in binary mode.
Output names are formed from the input path as written, so drive
letters and backslashes work, and the suffix test looks only at the text
after the last period.
A read-only input file is still deleted, and its output is also
read-only.
Paths may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR tar (1),
.BR zstd (1)
